            <div xml:id="d280">

                <pb facs="0402" n="394" xml:id="pg394"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/14364a: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Grew</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-08-25T20:00:00-04:00">August 25, 1941—8
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>535. There follows for your information extract from the Associated Press text
                    from London of Prime Minister Churchill’s radio address of August 24: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“But Europe is not the only continent to be tormented and devastated by
                            aggression. For five long years the Japanese military factions, seeking
                            to emulate the style of Hitler and Mussolini, taking all their posturing
                            as if it were a new European revelation, have been invading and harrying
                            the 500,000,000 inhabitants of China. Japanese armies have been
                            wandering about that vast land in futile excursions, carrying with them
                            carnage, ruin and corruption, and calling it ‘the Chinese incident.’
                            Now, they stretch a grasping hand into the southern seas of China. They
                            snatch Indochina from the wretched Vichy French. They menace by their
                            movements Siam, menace Singapore, the British link with Australasia, and
                            menace the Philippine Islands, under the protection of the United
                            States.</p>
                        <p>It is certain that this has got to stop. Every effort will be made to
                            secure a peaceful settlement. The United States are laboring with
                            infinite patience to arrive at a fair and amicable settlement which will
                            give Japan the utmost reassurance for her legitimate interests. We
                            earnestly hope these negotiations will succeed. But this I must say:
                            That if these hopes should fail we shall, of course, range ourselves
                            unhesitatingly at the side of the United States.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
